  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1039 AM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nThe region remains socked in with low stratus this morning. Adjusted \ntemperatures accordingly through tonight with stratus, drizzle, and \nfog in mind. Wednesday is a bit trickier, with some clearing ahead \nof the next low expected. However, morning fog and persistent, if \nhigher, cloud cover in the east prompted a lean toward \nConsShort/HRRR/ConsAll over NBM. Some of the fog may be dense on the \nedges of the stratus in central SD, but pinpointing exact extent of \nthe lowest visibilities is too low confidence right now for any \nthought of a headline. Generally, though, vsby down to 1/2 to 2 \nmiles will be widespread. \n\nA weak low stretches northeast to southwest near the Coteau by \nWednesday afternoon with some shortwave energy trailing over central \nSD. Both of these features will produce some rain showers. Can't \nrule out a rumble of thunder across the east along the front, \neither. Drier mid levels do move in behind this front, but high \npressure at the sfc and the latent moisture from recent rain will \nlikely lead to additional fog Wednesday night into Thursday morning \nbefore clearing. \n\nFriday night into Saturday, a Plains low pushes some additional \nprecipitation into the region. Another break is expected on Sunday \nbefore yet another low pressure system potentially affects the \nregion Monday into Tuesday. Forecast temperatures are warm enough to \nsupport all rain over the next week.",
